efined sugars  and the reprocessed foods the chips the  desserts and so forth because they  increase the risk for type 2 diabetes  which doubles a person's risk for  Alzheimer's disease now we've got a  challenge when it comes to nutrition and  brain health if you look at the  evolution of it we're going in a certain  direction  the bad news is that obesity and  overweight actually obesity quadruples a  risk for Alzheimer's but the good news  they've been doing studies of obese  individuals and they find when they lose  weight their memory abilities return in  a matter of months and they follow these  people over time and there are sustained  benefits over the years but as a  challenge you know the neuroscientists  have looked at what goes on in the brain  when we're trying to resist those  cookies and crackers and in this slide  you see some of the brain areas in the  frontal lobe that are controlling these  impulses so there's one part of my brain  that tells me when I see that second  piece of chocolate cake don't eat it  don't eat it but there's another part  right here that's saying eat the cake  eat the cake so it's like a battle of  the brain the good news is if we can  resist initially our neural circuits  actually change so through our own  willpower we can rewire our brains and  it gets easier over time and all of us  know if we've gone on a diet or a  healthy program it's hard to change the  habits initially but over time it gets  easier now there is good news on the  nutrition front it turns out that  alcohol in moderation is associated with  better brain health okay here's some  cheers in the back but then there's  always the question well how much is the  one glass of wine is it like this and  we're not sure why it works it may be  that having a glass of wine at dinner  relaxes us and that helps us with our  brain health or could be chemicals in  the wine one chemical it's called  resveratrol  and it's its anti-aging in the  laboratory so the scientists have  extracted the resveratrol out of the red  wine put it into capsules and now  they're testing it to see if it helps  with Alzheimer's the the challenges were  not sure that the capsule resveratrol  gets into the brain so if anybody's  taking resveratrol capsules just make  sure you wash it down with a nice  Bordeaux  now moderation with caffeine coffee and  tea that's also associated with better  brain health and we're also studying  nutrition we're looking at nutritional  supplements that could help the brain  we've studied pomegranate juice  pomegranate extract we just finished a  study of curcumin which is from turmeric  it's a spice it's it's in encourage  foods in India they consume a lot of  spicy foods they have a lower rate of  Alzheimer's but more importantly it  turns out in the laboratory the curcumin  is anti-inflammatory and it also fights  off those amyloid plaques so we should  be publishing our results this year more  to come in the meantime you might want  to just eat some Indian food every so  often it turns out that people who eat  Indian food more frequently perform  better on memory tests and you'd only  have to eat while you're taking the test  it's fantastic so what else can we do  there's a lot of other things of course  you want to avoid head trauma that's not  good for the brain anybody who's hit  their head and lost consciousness for an  hour or more that doubles their risk for  Alzheimer's you want to stop smoking if  you smoke not not good for your brain  stay positive  keeping a positive outlook helps us live  longer helps us live better and helps  our brain health and then finally you  know get a good doctor listen your  doctor simple things if you have high  blood pressure if you have high  cholesterol just taking your medicine  for the for those problems will improve  your brain health and also help you live  longer now question often comes up can  we prevent Alzheimer's disease and if if  you think of prevention as equivalent to  cure the answer is no but if we think of  prevention as staving off decline or  delaying the onset of symptoms I think  the answer is yes there have been a  number of studies showing that some of  these strategies  exercise eating a healthy diet reducing  stress can make a difference and can  delay the onset of symptoms by years and  we've done studies showing that there's  even an additive effect so if you do one  thing your brain is better if you do two  things it's even better so one way of  thinking about this a sedentary  lifestyle where you're eating all the  wrong things your brain may decline at  this rate but by living a brain healthy  strategy you could delay onset of  symptoms by many years and that can be  very meaningful in people's lives so the  issue is how do we help people change  when people come to see me at UCLA often  you know I'll do the standard medical  approach but also I'll ask them about  their diet and I may say well I want you  to eat this I want you to exercise and  often the response is well well doc  don't you have a pill you know so it's  it's hard to change and I think there's  there is a formula to this there are  things we can do we've tried to  integrate that into our books and I say  our because I want to say my wife Gigi  borgin is a co-author of all my books  and she actually if it weren't for Gigi  the books would have three four sections  introduction methods results and  discussion and no longer Rita but she's  able to make it accessible to a much  wider audience and I appreciate that but  you have to make the information  accessible so the first step is helping  people understand the connection between  brain health and behavior and if we  understand that we're more likely to  make the right decisions we also need to  create programs that are fun and easy my  my latest book is called two weeks to a  younger brain trust me if I'd called it  two years to a younger brain no one  would buy it but two weeks is enough  time to take baby steps and introduce  yourself to these kinds of strategies  and also enough time so you start seeing  results in experiencing results so you  feel better and that's what truly  motivates people for the long haul so  what about the future where are we going  well one of the things that we're  looking at it UCLA is trying to again  look at incentives to get people to  engage in these sorts of programs so  there have been studies looking at these  modifiable risk factors that I've been  talking about modifiable risk factors  for dementia and it turns out they have  a huge impact one study found that up to  half of all Alzheimer cases worldwide  could be attributable to these factors  so one of the studies we're doing right  now we call the UCLA Alzheimer's  prevention project and we're we're  recruiting people actually from the  electronic medical record and we're  getting them on a four hour per week  healthy lifestyle program which is very  inexpensive and what we have a control  group what we ultimately won't a'mma to  show is that these programs save the  health system dollars and if we can do  that then we can export it to lots of  health systems and motivate people in  that way through their health system so  anybody want to volunteer their story  here's a brave woman your look no I want  to hear the story I want to hear your  creative okay wait what why don't you  tell me the story and I'll repeat it  the professor sitting on the beach  he had just ridden a horse he's carrying  a teddy bear okay met sheep so so the  professor met a nun smoking a cigar who  was eating pasta under the palm tree  okay that I think that did deserve a  hand that was a fantastic story  well I I just have a quiet did you the  nun what hold on the nun was smoking the  cigar did did you go to Catholic school  okay so you can see how you can have a  lot of fun with these kinds of  approaches our UCLA longevity Center we  have programs for those sale you did  with names and f